<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Tall Tree! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Tall Tree!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple greetings on cards. Interactive: Write very simple sentences with assistance, even if errors are present.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple greetings or nature-related phrases on cards (e.g., "Happy garden day!"). Can write simple sentences about the garden or nature with teacher support (e.g., "This is a tall tree"). Can label pictures of plants or trees using simple words like "flowers," "trees," and "leaves".</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the writing focus for the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks them to stand up. The teacher shows flashcards of "garden," "tree," "flower," "leaf," "plant," "sun." Students repeat the words. Teacher leads a short "Garden Action Song" (e.g., "I plant a seed, I water it, it grows so tall, a tall tree!") with simple TPR gestures. This reactivates vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a large picture of a garden with a tall tree. "Look! It's our garden. What do you see?" (Points to tree). "This is a tree. A tall tree!" (Writes "This is a tall tree." on a large whiteboard or chart paper). "Look! I can write it. This is a tall tree." Teacher then points to a flower. "What is this?" "A flower. What color is the flower?" "Pink!" "The flower is pink." (Writes "The flower is pink." below the first sentence). Teacher models writing each word clearly, emphasizing letter formation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a small tree or a tall tree?" (Points to the written sentence and the picture) - Expected answer: "A tall tree!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is the flower?" (Points to the written sentence and the picture) - Expected answer: "Pink!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards with pictures of garden items (tree, flower, leaf) and the corresponding word written clearly below. Chart paper with simple sentences and labels about a garden. A sample greeting card that says "Happy Garden Day!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about nature: Describe plants and trees with singular phrases (e.g., "The tree is tall."). Asking questions in nature: Use basic questions like "What color is the flower?" to learn about plants. Sociolinguistic: Respect for nature: Talking about plants and trees using respectful language (e.g., "I like plants.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays flashcards: a picture of a tree with the word "tree" below it, a flower with "flower," a leaf with "leaf." Students chorally repeat the words, focusing on /t/ in "tree" and /l/ in "leaf."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a chart with simple sentences and pictures:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a tree) "This is a tree."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a tall tree) "This is a tall tree."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a pink flower) "The flower is pink."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a green leaf) "The leaf is green."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher models writing a short greeting: "Look! I can write a card for our garden! 'Happy Garden Day!'" Teacher writes the greeting clearly on a small card.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word is this?" (Teacher points to "tree" on the chart). Students say "tree."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which sentence talks about the pink flower?" (Teacher points to different sentences; students identify "The flower is pink.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing. Guided practice to build confidence in written expression.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Noun + verb + adjective (e.g., "The flowers are pink."). Present simple (e.g., "This is a tall tree."). Vocabulary: garden, tree, flower, plant, leaf, pink, green, tall. Demonstrative: this.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Labeling Pictures (Controlled Practice):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple pictures of a tree, a flower, and a leaf. Below each picture, there is a blank line.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are provided with a word bank at the top: "tree," "flower," "leaf."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write the correct word to label each picture. Teacher circulates, assisting with letter formation and word recognition. (Focus on writing familiar vocabulary, practicing /g/ in garden if included, /t/ in tree, and /s/ in sun if labeling the sun.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Sentence Completion (Semi-controlled):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a second worksheet with partially completed sentences and corresponding pictures.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a tall tree) "This is a ________ tree." (Word choice: tall, short)</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a green leaf) "The leaf is ________." (Word choice: green, pink)</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Picture of a flower) "This is a ________." (Word choice: flower, plant)</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose the correct word from the options provided and write it in the blank. Teacher assists with reading the options and writing the words.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Guided Sentence Writing (Interactive Peer Check):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are given a picture of a simple garden scene (e.g., a tall tree, pink flowers).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher writes sentence starters on the board: "This is a _____." "The _____ is _____." "I like my _____."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one or two sentence starters and write a simple sentence about the picture, using words from the previous activities.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After writing, students turn to a partner and show them their sentence(s). Partners can point to the picture and the words, practicing the names of the items.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual writing of a short message or description on a "garden card."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: My Garden Card!</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes small paper cards (pre-folded to resemble greeting cards) and crayons.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are asked to draw their favorite plant or a scene from "Our Garden" on the front of the card (e.g., a tall tree, a pink flower).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">On the inside of the card, students will write a simple greeting or a sentence about their drawing. The teacher writes options on the board for support:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Happy Garden Day!" (simple greeting)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is a tall tree." (describing their drawing)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The flower is pink." (describing their drawing)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like my garden." (expressing respect for nature)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one or two sentences to write on their card. Teacher circulates, providing individual assistance with writing, spelling, and sentence structure, encouraging them to try their best.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finished cards can be displayed on a "Class Garden Wall."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check learning and provide feedback.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of student "Garden Cards."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "Garden Cards" with their drawing and written message.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted to write a greeting or sentence (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least one target vocabulary word (e.g., tree, flower, garden, tall, pink, green) (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Wrote a sentence related to the drawing/theme (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of writing (Needs support / Fair / Good)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked to verbally respond or show a thumbs up/down before leaving:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write 'This is a tree'?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I like writing about our garden?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did I write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of my garden card do I like best?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the writing activities worked well for 1st graders? (e.g., Sentence starters, word banks, visual support).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future writing lessons to support students struggling with letter formation or sentence construction? (e.g., more tracing practice, individual whiteboards for quick writing, pairing stronger writers with those who need more support).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students meet the objective of writing simple greetings or sentences with assistance?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration):</b> In the next lesson, students will share their garden cards with a small group and explain their drawing and writing, summarizing their learning about the garden and preparing for a collaborative project related to gardening and nature conservation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>